= Ontology Versioning and Transition Framework
Svein Harald Olsen
:revnumber: 1.0
:revdate: 2026-04-21
:toc: left
:toclevels: 4
:sectnums:

== Scope

This specification defines the rules and structure for managing multiple versions of the Network Code (NC) ontology, including:

- Persistent and version-specific namespaces
- Coexistence of multiple ontology versions
- Semantic alignment across versions
- Explicit directional transitions between versions
- Support for a single knowledge graph across versions

The objective is to support *semantic interoperability across time*.

== Conformance Language

The key words *"shall"*, *"should"*, and *"may"* are to be interpreted as described in RFC 2119.

== Namespace Requirements

=== Persistent Namespace

The following namespace shall represent the current version of the NC ontology:

----
https://cim4.eu/ns/nc#
----

- This namespace shall always resolve to the current version
- At present, it corresponds to NC 2.4

=== Version-Specific Namespaces

Each version shall have a stable namespace:

----
https://cim4.eu/ns/nc/2.3#
https://cim4.eu/ns/nc/2.4#
https://cim4.eu/ns/nc/2.5#
----

- These namespaces shall remain persistent and resolvable
- They shall not change over time

== Ontology Publication Requirements

=== Full Ontology Requirement

Each version (nc23, nc24, nc25) shall be published as a complete ontology.

A complete ontology shall include:

- All classes
- All properties
- All enumerations
- All constraints and semantic definitions

Partial ontologies shall not be used.

== Knowledge Graph Requirement

The system shall support a single knowledge graph containing:

- Instance data
- nc23, nc24, nc25 ontologies
- Alignment ontology
- Transition ontologies

The system shall not require duplication of instance data.

== Common Alignment Ontology

=== Purpose

A common alignment ontology shall define semantic equivalence across versions.

=== Scope

The alignment ontology shall include only:

- Semantically identical classes
- Semantically identical properties

=== Semantics

The following predicates shall be used:

- owl:equivalentClass
- owl:equivalentProperty
- skos:exactMatch
- skos:closeMatch

== Transition Ontologies

=== Required Transitions

The following transitions shall be defined:

- nc23 → nc24
- nc24 → nc23
- nc24 → nc25
- nc25 → nc24

=== Directionality

Transitions shall be directional.

Reverse mappings shall be explicitly defined.

== Change Classification

The following change types shall be supported:

- identical
- renamed
- deprecated
- replaced
- split
- merged
- nonBreakingDefinitionChange
- breakingDefinitionChange
- added
- removed

== Transition Semantics

Each mapping shall include:

- Source term
- Target term(s)
- Mapping type
- Lossless or lossy indicator
- Human-readable explanation

Optionally:

- Transformation rules
- Validation constraints

== Examples

=== Same Class Across Versions

[source,turtle]
----
@prefix nc23: <https://cim4.eu/ns/nc/2.3#> .
@prefix nc24: <https://cim4.eu/ns/nc/2.4#> .
@prefix nc25: <https://cim4.eu/ns/nc/2.5#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .

nc23:GridConnectionPoint
    owl:equivalentClass nc24:GridConnectionPoint ,
                        nc25:GridConnectionPoint .
----

=== Same Property Across Versions

[source,turtle]
----
nc23:IdentifiedObject.name
    owl:equivalentProperty nc24:IdentifiedObject.name ,
                           nc25:IdentifiedObject.name .
----

=== Non-Breaking Definition Change

[source,turtle]
----
@prefix nct: <https://cim4.eu/ns/nc/transition#> .

[] a nct:ClassMapping ;
   nct:sourceTerm nc24:BoundaryPoint ;
   nct:targetTerm nc25:BoundaryPoint ;
   nct:mappingKind nct:nonBreakingDefinitionChange ;
   nct:isLossless true ;
   rdfs:comment "Clarified definition to include substation-internal boundaries." .
----

=== Breaking Definition Change

[source,turtle]
----
[] a nct:ClassMapping ;
   nct:sourceTerm nc24:EnergyExchangePoint ;
   nct:targetTerm nc25:EnergyExchangePoint ;
   nct:mappingKind nct:breakingDefinitionChange ;
   nct:isLossless false ;
   rdfs:comment "Scope narrowed to inter-TSO exchanges only." .
----

=== Attribute Renamed

[source,turtle]
----
[] a nct:PropertyMapping ;
   nct:sourceTerm nc24:AgentPost.role ;
   nct:targetTerm nc25:AgentPost.function ;
   nct:mappingKind nct:renamed ;
   nct:isLossless true ;
   rdfs:comment "Renamed for clarity; semantics unchanged." .
----

=== Class Extended in Next Version

[source,turtle]
----
[] a nct:ClassMapping ;
   nct:sourceTerm nc24:CommonResponsibilityPoint ;
   nct:targetTerm nc25:CommonResponsibilityPoint ;
   nct:mappingKind nct:added ;
   nct:isLossless true ;
   rdfs:comment "Added attributes: isDirectCurrent, geometryRef." .
----

=== Split Example

[source,turtle]
----
[] a nct:ClassMapping ;
   nct:sourceTerm nc24:PowerSchedule ;
   nct:targetTerm nc25:PlannedSchedule ,
                  nc25:OperationalSchedule ;
   nct:mappingKind nct:split ;
   nct:isLossless false ;
   rdfs:comment "Split into planning and operational contexts." .
----

=== Reverse Mapping (nc25 → nc24)

[source,turtle]
----
[] a nct:ClassMapping ;
   nct:sourceTerm nc25:PlannedSchedule ;
   nct:targetTerm nc24:PowerSchedule ;
   nct:mappingKind nct:merged ;
   nct:isLossless true ;
   rdfs:comment "Merged back into nc24 structure." .
----

== Architectural Principle

Versioning shall be achieved by:

- Maintaining multiple ontologies
- Defining explicit semantic mappings
- Avoiding duplication of instance data

This enables one knowledge graph to support multiple temporal interpretations.

== Conformance Requirements

An implementation shall:

1. Publish full ontologies for nc23, nc24, nc25
2. Maintain persistent namespace nc for current version
3. Provide a common alignment ontology
4. Provide directional transition ontologies
5. Support a single shared knowledge graph
6. Avoid dataset duplication
7. Ensure resolvable versioned URIs
8. Provide machine-readable mappings

== Recommendations

The framework should support:

- Human-readable change documentation
- Machine-readable transformation rules
- Validation before and after transition
- Provenance tracking of transformations
- Explainable version differences

Relevant technologies include:

- OWL / RDFS
- SKOS
- SHACL
- PROV-O
- LinkML